GTIG reports the first zero-day exploit it believes was developed with AI, prepared by criminals for a planned mass-exploitation event that its counter-discovery may have prevented, not an exploit confirmed in use in the wild; the 2FA bypass also requires valid user credentials ([GTIG, 2026-05-11](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/ai-vulnerability-exploitation-initial-access)). CANFAIL and LONGSTREAM do not call an LLM at runtime: they carry LLM-generated decoy code to obfuscate their functionality, and the report's example of malware driven by an LLM at runtime is the Android backdoor PROMPTSPY ([GTIG, 2026-05-11](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/ai-vulnerability-exploitation-initial-access)). UNC5673 overlaps with TEMP.Hex rather than being it, and uses relay tooling to pool LLM accounts; the report does not describe stolen LLM API keys as a primary objective or a criminal market for them, and its only point on AI API secrets is that the LiteLLM compromise could expose them. TeamPCP entered through compromised PyPI packages and malicious pull requests to GitHub repositories, and embedded SANDCLOCK to take AWS keys and GitHub tokens from build environments ([GTIG, 2026-05-11](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/ai-vulnerability-exploitation-initial-access)).
